## Step 3 — Write NOVA.md

Use this exact structure. **Omit any section where you found nothing relevant.**

```markdown
# NOVA.md

**<one sentence: what this project does and who uses it>**

## Commands

| Task  | Command             |
|-------|---------------------|
| Dev   | `<exact command>`   |
| Test  | `<exact command>`   |
| Lint  | `<exact command>`   |
| Build | `<exact command>`   |

## Architecture

- <key design decision the agent must understand>
- <important module: what it does and when to touch it>
- <technology choice with practical implication for the agent>

## Code Conventions

- <naming rule — e.g. "snake_case for all Python identifiers">
- <import rule — e.g. "stdlib → third-party → local, blank lines between groups">
- <pattern to follow — e.g. "always use Path not os.path">
- <pattern to avoid — e.g. "never use print() — use the logger">

## Development Workflow

- <how to add a new feature — which files to touch and in what order>
- <how to run a single test — exact command with flags>
- <how to lint / type-check before committing>

## Guardrails

- **NEVER** modify `<path>` — <reason>
- **NEVER** run `<command>` — <reason>
- **NEVER** commit `<file pattern>` — <reason>

## Key Files

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `<path>` | <what it does — one line> |
| `<path>` | <what it does — one line> |
```

